WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.976
How do welding settings relate to fatigue life?

2
00:00:03.662 --> 00:00:08.334
This statistical modelling project uses twenty-seven observations from a three-factor coursework

3
00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:09.151
dataset.

4
00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:14.256
The work connects regression diagnostics with interpretable effects and predictions inside the

5
00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:15.244
study range.

6
00:00:16.817 --> 00:00:20.704
The factors are welding current, voltage, and electrode diameter.

7
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.276
Each has three coded levels.

8
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:28.951
I first fitted a full quadratic model, then examined residuals, transformed fatigue life to

9
00:00:28.951 --> 00:00:31.201
a log scale, and simplified the model.

10
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.791
These original figures show residuals for full quadratic fits before and after taking logs.

11
00:00:38.477 --> 00:00:41.175
The aim is to inspect patterns and changing spread.

12
00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:47.389
Smaller numerical residuals on a different scale do not, by themselves, establish a better model.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.644
The selected model keeps the three main effects on log fatigue life.

14
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:57.751
This newly calculated diagnostic uses the original twenty-seven observations.

15
00:00:58.439 --> 00:01:02.897
It compares observed and fitted log responses and shows the remaining residual pattern.

16
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.658
The final model has R squared point nine six five eight.

17
00:01:08.344 --> 00:01:12.681
Training root mean squared error is point one seven one three on the log scale.

18
00:01:13.368 --> 00:01:18.684
Leave-one-out error is point two zero one four, holding the chosen model fixed rather than repeating

19
00:01:18.684 --> 00:01:20.776
model selection inside each fold.

20
00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:26.771
Holding the other factors fixed, a two point five amp increase in current corresponds to an

21
00:01:26.771 --> 00:01:31.520
estimated eight point six eight percent rise in fatigue life on the geometric mean scale.

22
00:01:32.206 --> 00:01:36.769
The confidence interval for this fitted effect runs from seven point seven zero to nine point

23
00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:37.999
six seven percent.

24
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:44.292
The largest fitted value inside the studied region is approximately seven thousand two hundred

25
00:01:44.292 --> 00:01:49.855
and thirteen cycles, at three hundred amps, twenty-five volts and two point five millimetres.

26
00:01:50.541 --> 00:01:53.567
This is a model prediction on the geometric mean scale.

27
00:01:54.254 --> 00:01:57.535
Independent confirmation runs would test the process choice.
